INT. VICTORIAN PARLOR - EVENING

1893. Gaslight, heavy drapes, a piano no one has touched in years. A dinner is being given and nothing has been eaten.

LADY CORDELIA ASHWORTH, 50s, black silk, sits very straight at the head of a small table. Beside her, her husband SIR REGINALD ASHWORTH, 60s, whiskers waxed, studies a fork. Opposite, their niece PHILIPPA MARLOWE, 20s, pale blue gown, turns her water glass a quarter turn and back. Next to her, the family solicitor MR. EZEKIEL FROST, 50s, spectacles, holds a folded paper he has not yet unfolded. By the sideboard stands the butler HARGREAVES, 60s, white gloves, expressionless. And at the foot of the table, hat still on, sits CAPTAIN JULIAN VANE, 30s, in a regimental coat with one button missing.

A clock ticks. Nobody lifts a spoon.

LADY CORDELIA
Hargreaves. The soup is cold.

HARGREAVES
It was cold when it was served, madam.

LADY CORDELIA
Then take it away.

Hargreaves does not move. Sir Reginald sets the fork down. Philippa stares at the captain's missing button.

SIR REGINALD
Frost. You had something to read.

MR. FROST
I had, Sir Reginald. After dinner.

SIR REGINALD
There is no dinner.

MR. FROST
Quite.

Mr. Frost unfolds the paper. He smooths it twice. Captain Vane takes off his hat and sets it, crown down, beside his plate. Philippa lifts her eyes to the ceiling.

A bell rings in the hall. The house telephone, a new brass horn on the wall, rattles.

Hargreaves slips out to the hall. The receiver is lifted. His voice carries back through the open door.

HARGREAVES (O.S.)
Yes. Yes. I will convey it.

The receiver is set down with great care. He returns.

LADY CORDELIA
Well?

HARGREAVES
The constable, madam. He is at the gate.

Captain Vane stands. His chair scrapes. Sir Reginald does not look at him.

PHILIPPA
(low)
Julian.

CAPTAIN VANE
Sit down, Pippa.

She does not sit down, because she has not stood. She holds the glass. Lady Cordelia dabs her mouth with a napkin that has touched nothing.

LADY CORDELIA
Reginald, the paper.

SIR REGINALD
Frost.

MR. FROST
(reading)
"I, Hartley Ashworth, being of sound mind--"

CAPTAIN VANE
Stop.

MR. FROST
Captain, I must.

CAPTAIN VANE
Not that one. The other.

Mr. Frost turns the page over. There is a second sheet beneath it. He reads it silently. His face loses color. He passes it to Sir Reginald, who reads and passes it to his wife.

Lady Cordelia folds it into a square, then a smaller square, then places it in the candle flame.

The paper curls and blackens. Nobody breathes.

HARGREAVES
(at the door, quietly)
Shall I admit the constable, madam?

LADY CORDELIA
Not yet.

She takes up her spoon. She tastes the cold soup. She sets the spoon down.

LADY CORDELIA (CONT'D)
Now.
